1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,625
You walk into a room...

2
00:00:01,665 --> 00:00:04,129
and for one awful second, you're certain

3
00:00:04,169 --> 00:00:06,697
everyone saw it. The coffee stain on your

4
00:00:06,737 --> 00:00:09,201
shirt. The little trip on the doorframe.

5
00:00:09,241 --> 00:00:12,033
The thing you said just a bit too loud.

6
00:00:13,083 --> 00:00:14,598
That feeling has a name.

7
00:00:14,638 --> 00:00:17,125
And in the year two thousand, a team of

8
00:00:17,165 --> 00:00:20,105
psychologists at Cornell University decided to

9
00:00:20,145 --> 00:00:21,595
stop guessing about it...

10
00:00:21,635 --> 00:00:22,633
and count.

11
00:00:23,683 --> 00:00:25,599
They put students in a shirt they'd be

12
00:00:25,639 --> 00:00:27,761
embarrassed to wear, sent them into a room

13
00:00:27,801 --> 00:00:30,026
full of other students, and asked one simple

14
00:00:30,066 --> 00:00:32,629
question. How many people actually noticed?

15
00:00:33,679 --> 00:00:36,174
The answer was about half of what the students

16
00:00:36,214 --> 00:00:38,544
expected. And the reason why says something

17
00:00:38,584 --> 00:00:40,858
strange about where you're standing, every

18
00:00:40,898 --> 00:00:43,007
time you picture yourself through other

19
00:00:43,047 --> 00:00:44,169
people's eyes.

20
00:00:50,019 --> 00:00:52,272
The researchers were Thomas Gilovich, Victoria

21
00:00:52,312 --> 00:00:53,667
Medvec and Kenneth Savitsky.

22
00:00:53,707 --> 00:00:55,810
And first, they needed a shirt that college

23
00:00:55,850 --> 00:00:58,443
students really would not want to be seen in.

24
00:00:59,493 --> 00:01:02,360
They tested the idea on students first, and

25
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:05,402
landed on a T-shirt with a big picture of the

26
00:01:05,442 --> 00:01:06,822
singer Barry Manilow.

27
00:01:06,862 --> 00:01:08,309
Nothing against Barry.

28
00:01:08,349 --> 00:01:11,419
In that place, at that time, students rated it

29
00:01:11,459 --> 00:01:13,431
as embarrassing to wear.

30
00:01:14,481 --> 00:01:16,891
Why a shirt? Because it's something everyone

31
00:01:16,931 --> 00:01:19,062
in the room could check for themselves.

32
00:01:19,102 --> 00:01:21,233
No guessing about thoughts or feelings.

33
00:01:21,273 --> 00:01:23,349
Either you saw the face on that shirt...

34
00:01:23,389 --> 00:01:24,518
or you didn't.

35
00:01:25,568 --> 00:01:26,660
Here's how it worked.

36
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:28,277
One student puts on the shirt.

37
00:01:28,317 --> 00:01:30,757
They're led into a room where a group of other

38
00:01:30,797 --> 00:01:32,967
students are already sitting, filling out

39
00:01:33,007 --> 00:01:35,177
questionnaires. They're there for a short

40
00:01:35,217 --> 00:01:37,669
while… and then they're taken back out.

41
00:01:38,719 --> 00:01:39,859
Now comes the question.

42
00:01:39,899 --> 00:01:42,167
How many of the people in that room could say

43
00:01:42,207 --> 00:01:43,685
who was on your shirt?

44
00:01:44,735 --> 00:01:46,842
Put yourself in those shoes for a second.

45
00:01:46,882 --> 00:01:49,147
You just walked in with a giant face on your

46
00:01:49,187 --> 00:01:51,294
chest. Heads turned when the door opened.

47
00:01:51,334 --> 00:01:52,836
Of course they saw it.

48
00:01:53,886 --> 00:01:56,052
The wearers guessed that close to half the

49
00:01:56,092 --> 00:01:58,258
room would be able to name the face on the

50
00:01:58,298 --> 00:01:58,963
shirt.

51
00:02:00,013 --> 00:02:02,117
Then the researchers asked the room.

52
00:02:02,157 --> 00:02:03,937
… About a quarter could.

53
00:02:04,987 --> 00:02:07,179
Close to half… versus about a quarter.

54
00:02:07,219 --> 00:02:09,351
The wearers had roughly doubled their

55
00:02:09,391 --> 00:02:11,759
audience. Most of the people in that room

56
00:02:11,799 --> 00:02:14,557
either didn't notice, or didn't remember.

57
00:02:15,607 --> 00:02:17,834
And remember, these people were sitting right

58
00:02:17,874 --> 00:02:19,899
there, a few feet away, with nothing more

59
00:02:19,939 --> 00:02:21,662
interesting in front of them than a

60
00:02:21,702 --> 00:02:22,757
questionnaire.

61
00:02:23,807 --> 00:02:25,934
The researchers called it the spotlight

62
00:02:25,974 --> 00:02:28,156
effect: the feeling that a beam of light

63
00:02:28,196 --> 00:02:30,712
follows you around, when most of the time, the

64
00:02:30,752 --> 00:02:32,656
audience is looking somewhere else.

65
00:02:32,696 --> 00:02:34,656
But that raised an obvious question.

66
00:02:34,696 --> 00:02:36,824
Was it only about embarrassment?

67
00:02:40,474 --> 00:02:42,271
Maybe we only inflate the bad stuff.

68
00:02:42,311 --> 00:02:44,006
Fear makes everything look bigger.

69
00:02:44,046 --> 00:02:46,386
So the team ran it again, with a twist.

70
00:02:47,436 --> 00:02:49,720
This time, students wore a shirt they actually

71
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:52,045
liked: one with Bob Marley, Martin Luther King

72
00:02:52,085 --> 00:02:54,015
Junior, or the comedian Jerry Seinfeld.

73
00:02:54,055 --> 00:02:56,174
A shirt you might be proud to wear.

74
00:02:57,224 --> 00:02:59,032
And the same thing happened.

75
00:02:59,072 --> 00:03:01,739
The wearers overestimated how many people

76
00:03:01,779 --> 00:03:03,653
noticed. Pride didn't fix it.

77
00:03:03,693 --> 00:03:06,882
The spotlight shines on the good stuff too.

78
00:03:07,932 --> 00:03:10,658
Then they left the shirts behind and tried

79
00:03:10,698 --> 00:03:12,634
something closer to real life

80
00:03:12,674 --> 00:03:15,400
group discussions. People talked through a

81
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:18,429
topic together, and afterward, each person was

82
00:03:18,469 --> 00:03:21,261
asked how much their own comments had stood

83
00:03:21,301 --> 00:03:22,776
out. The clever points...

84
00:03:22,816 --> 00:03:24,549
and the awkward ones.

85
00:03:25,599 --> 00:03:27,853
Again, people believed their own moments were

86
00:03:27,893 --> 00:03:30,199
more noticeable to the others than they really

87
00:03:30,239 --> 00:03:32,391
were. The great line you were proud of, and

88
00:03:32,431 --> 00:03:34,482
the clumsy one you've been replaying ever

89
00:03:34,522 --> 00:03:36,962
since… both landed softer than you think.

90
00:03:38,012 --> 00:03:40,857
A couple of years later, in two thousand two

91
00:03:40,897 --> 00:03:43,614
Gilovich and his colleagues pushed the idea

92
00:03:43,654 --> 00:03:46,371
further. Do other people notice our ups and

93
00:03:46,411 --> 00:03:47,781
downs? A bad hair day.

94
00:03:47,821 --> 00:03:50,736
A bad game. A bad round of a video game?

95
00:03:51,786 --> 00:03:53,949
Across three studies, people kept

96
00:03:53,989 --> 00:03:56,885
overestimating how much others noticed those

97
00:03:56,925 --> 00:03:59,755
swings. To you, Tuesday's hair is a crisis.

98
00:03:59,795 --> 00:04:01,557
To everyone else, you look...

99
00:04:01,597 --> 00:04:02,548
like you.

100
00:04:03,598 --> 00:04:05,903
Think about what that means on an ordinary

101
00:04:05,943 --> 00:04:08,137
day. You notice the pimple, the wrinkled

102
00:04:08,177 --> 00:04:10,427
sleeve, the word you mispronounced in the

103
00:04:10,467 --> 00:04:12,940
meeting. Each one feels like front page news.

104
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:15,508
To the people around you, it's a tiny dot in a

105
00:04:15,548 --> 00:04:17,462
busy picture… gone by lunch.

106
00:04:18,512 --> 00:04:20,847
So it isn't only shame, and it isn't only

107
00:04:20,887 --> 00:04:23,163
pride. Something more basic is going on.

108
00:04:23,203 --> 00:04:25,827
And the explanation is a little uncomfortable

109
00:04:25,867 --> 00:04:28,086
because the problem isn't other people.

110
00:04:28,126 --> 00:04:30,445
It's where your brain is standing.

111
00:04:34,095 --> 00:04:37,055
Here's the idea. When you try to imagine how

112
00:04:37,095 --> 00:04:39,782
you look to others, you don't start from

113
00:04:39,822 --> 00:04:42,781
nothing. You start from your own experience.

114
00:04:42,821 --> 00:04:46,103
And your own experience of you is enormous.

115
00:04:47,153 --> 00:04:48,889
You feel the shirt the entire time.

116
00:04:48,929 --> 00:04:50,158
You know about the stain.

117
00:04:50,198 --> 00:04:51,731
You heard your own voice crack.

118
00:04:51,771 --> 00:04:53,812
You've been living inside that moment, in

119
00:04:53,852 --> 00:04:55,994
close-up, with the sound turned all the way

120
00:04:56,034 --> 00:04:56,536
up.

121
00:04:57,586 --> 00:04:59,701
Psychologists call this anchoring and

122
00:04:59,741 --> 00:05:02,030
adjustment. You anchor on your own vivid

123
00:05:02,070 --> 00:05:04,476
experience, then you try to adjust for the

124
00:05:04,516 --> 00:05:06,864
fact that other people aren't inside your

125
00:05:06,904 --> 00:05:08,728
head. The trouble is, we adjust...

126
00:05:08,768 --> 00:05:10,399
but not nearly enough.

127
00:05:11,449 --> 00:05:13,845
Picture standing on a stage under a bright

128
00:05:13,885 --> 00:05:16,513
light. From up there, the light is all you can

129
00:05:16,553 --> 00:05:19,123
see. From the seats, you're one small figure

130
00:05:19,163 --> 00:05:21,559
and most of the audience is thinking about

131
00:05:21,599 --> 00:05:22,819
something else.

132
00:05:23,869 --> 00:05:26,151
Two more experiments in that same paper

133
00:05:26,191 --> 00:05:27,758
supported this explanation.

134
00:05:27,798 --> 00:05:30,199
And it points to something that's easy to

135
00:05:30,239 --> 00:05:32,580
forget. Every person in that room has an

136
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:33,771
anchor of their own

137
00:05:33,811 --> 00:05:36,449
their own shirt, their own questionnaire, the

138
00:05:36,489 --> 00:05:37,878
thing they said earlier.

139
00:05:37,918 --> 00:05:40,497
Your spotlight is competing with a room full

140
00:05:40,537 --> 00:05:42,078
of other spotlights.

141
00:05:43,128 --> 00:05:45,804
There's an older experiment that shows just

142
00:05:45,844 --> 00:05:47,952
how strong the inside view can be.

143
00:05:47,992 --> 00:05:50,542
In nineteen eighty, at Dartmouth College

144
00:05:50,582 --> 00:05:53,322
researchers Richard Kleck and Angelo Strenta

145
00:05:53,362 --> 00:05:56,101
gave volunteers a realistic scar, painted on

146
00:05:56,141 --> 00:05:58,565
with makeup. Each volunteer saw it in a

147
00:05:58,605 --> 00:05:59,397
mirror.

148
00:06:00,447 --> 00:06:02,870
Then, just before they met a stranger for a

149
00:06:02,910 --> 00:06:05,448
conversation, the makeup artist came back for

150
00:06:05,488 --> 00:06:07,854
a quick touch up… and secretly removed the

151
00:06:07,894 --> 00:06:08,530
scar.

152
00:06:09,580 --> 00:06:11,872
The volunteers didn't know it was gone.

153
00:06:11,912 --> 00:06:14,622
And afterward, they reported that the stranger

154
00:06:14,662 --> 00:06:16,536
had reacted to their appearance.

155
00:06:16,576 --> 00:06:18,987
They felt reactions to a scar that wasn't

156
00:06:19,027 --> 00:06:19,736
there.

157
00:06:20,786 --> 00:06:23,262
It's a different study, about a different

158
00:06:23,302 --> 00:06:25,717
question. But it shows the same pattern.

159
00:06:25,757 --> 00:06:28,172
What we expect people to see can quietly

160
00:06:28,212 --> 00:06:30,465
rewrite what we think they saw.

161
00:06:31,515 --> 00:06:34,410
So the spotlight effect isn't vanity, and it

162
00:06:34,450 --> 00:06:36,478
isn't a flaw in your character.

163
00:06:36,518 --> 00:06:39,347
It's a side effect of being the only person

164
00:06:39,387 --> 00:06:41,805
who lives inside your own head.

165
00:06:45,455 --> 00:06:48,349
Okay. But sometimes people really do notice.

166
00:06:48,389 --> 00:06:49,683
You really did trip.

167
00:06:49,723 --> 00:06:51,617
Everybody really did hear it.

168
00:06:51,657 --> 00:06:52,674
What then?

169
00:06:53,724 --> 00:06:56,458
In two thousand one, Kenneth Savitsky

170
00:06:56,498 --> 00:06:59,451
Nicholas Epley and Thomas Gilovich tested

171
00:06:59,491 --> 00:07:02,445
exactly that. Across four studies, people

172
00:07:02,485 --> 00:07:05,730
imagined social blunders, or failed at a task

173
00:07:05,770 --> 00:07:08,869
in front of others, or were described in an

174
00:07:08,909 --> 00:07:10,500
embarrassing way.

175
00:07:11,550 --> 00:07:13,478
Each time, they expected to be judged more

176
00:07:13,518 --> 00:07:15,367
harshly than they actually were.

177
00:07:16,417 --> 00:07:17,351
Part of the reason

178
00:07:17,391 --> 00:07:19,453
when you're the one who messed up, you're

179
00:07:19,493 --> 00:07:20,684
locked onto the mistake.

180
00:07:20,724 --> 00:07:22,940
The people watching see more of the picture.

181
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:24,068
The question was hard.

182
00:07:24,108 --> 00:07:25,811
The room was loud. You seem tired.

183
00:07:25,851 --> 00:07:28,560
They give you context you don't give yourself.

184
00:07:29,610 --> 00:07:32,600
Think about the last time a friend tripped, or

185
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:34,577
lost their words in a meeting.

186
00:07:34,617 --> 00:07:36,355
Did you think less of them?

187
00:07:36,395 --> 00:07:38,200
Or did you think: rough day?

188
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:40,374
That's the view from the outside...

189
00:07:40,414 --> 00:07:42,345
and it's usually kinder.

190
00:07:43,395 --> 00:07:46,015
A few years earlier, the same team had found a

191
00:07:46,055 --> 00:07:48,212
cousin of this effect, the illusion of

192
00:07:48,252 --> 00:07:50,641
transparency. People overestimate how much

193
00:07:50,681 --> 00:07:52,144
their inner feelings show.

194
00:07:52,184 --> 00:07:54,168
Liars think their lies are obvious.

195
00:07:54,208 --> 00:07:56,828
People think their disgust is written all over

196
00:07:56,868 --> 00:07:58,895
their face. Mostly, it isn't.

197
00:07:59,945 --> 00:08:01,041
Now, an honest note.

198
00:08:01,081 --> 00:08:03,541
Most of these studies come from one group of

199
00:08:03,581 --> 00:08:05,756
researchers, often with small groups of

200
00:08:05,796 --> 00:08:08,255
college students, and the exact numbers move

201
00:08:08,295 --> 00:08:10,471
around. What holds up is the direction

202
00:08:10,511 --> 00:08:13,133
we think we're more visible than we are.

203
00:08:14,183 --> 00:08:15,258
And pressure matters.

204
00:08:15,298 --> 00:08:17,595
In a study at the University of Southampton

205
00:08:17,635 --> 00:08:20,038
the spotlight feeling got stronger when people

206
00:08:20,078 --> 00:08:21,684
felt they were being evaluated.

207
00:08:21,724 --> 00:08:23,490
That's why knowing about it helps...

208
00:08:23,530 --> 00:08:26,323
but doesn't switch the feeling off on its own.

209
00:08:29,973 --> 00:08:32,518
So what can you actually do with this?

210
00:08:32,558 --> 00:08:35,171
Here are three things, each tied to the

211
00:08:35,211 --> 00:08:36,173
research.

212
00:08:37,223 --> 00:08:39,639
One. Name it. In a two thousand three study

213
00:08:39,679 --> 00:08:42,150
Savitsky and Gilovich had people give a short

214
00:08:42,190 --> 00:08:44,382
speech. Some of them were told about the

215
00:08:44,422 --> 00:08:46,391
illusion of transparency beforehand

216
00:08:46,431 --> 00:08:48,846
that nerves show less than they feel.

217
00:08:49,896 --> 00:08:52,012
In that study, those speakers felt less

218
00:08:52,052 --> 00:08:54,555
nervous, and both they and the people watching

219
00:08:54,595 --> 00:08:56,269
rated their speeches as better.

220
00:08:56,309 --> 00:08:58,259
Just knowing about the effect made a

221
00:08:58,299 --> 00:08:59,257
difference.

222
00:09:00,307 --> 00:09:01,670
Two. Widen the frame.

223
00:09:01,710 --> 00:09:04,544
When you replay a mistake, picture what the

224
00:09:04,584 --> 00:09:07,350
other person saw: the whole situation, not

225
00:09:07,390 --> 00:09:09,221
just the worst second of it.

226
00:09:09,261 --> 00:09:11,827
That's what the blunder studies suggest

227
00:09:11,867 --> 00:09:13,687
onlookers actually do.

228
00:09:14,737 --> 00:09:16,195
Three. Count the room.

229
00:09:16,235 --> 00:09:19,122
Next time you're sure everyone noticed, try

230
00:09:19,162 --> 00:09:21,913
this: name three things you noticed about

231
00:09:21,953 --> 00:09:23,206
other people today.

232
00:09:23,246 --> 00:09:25,725
Most of us struggle to name even one.

233
00:09:25,765 --> 00:09:28,788
That's your evidence that their attention was

234
00:09:28,828 --> 00:09:29,791
busy too.

235
00:09:30,841 --> 00:09:33,433
So, three things: name it, widen the frame

236
00:09:33,473 --> 00:09:35,942
count the room. The beam doesn't go away.

237
00:09:35,982 --> 00:09:38,391
You just stop mistaking it for the whole

238
00:09:38,431 --> 00:09:39,148
stage.

239
00:09:40,198 --> 00:09:42,685
None of this replaces real help for serious

240
00:09:42,725 --> 00:09:45,211
anxiety. If the feeling of being watched is

241
00:09:45,251 --> 00:09:47,796
running your life, that's worth talking to a

242
00:09:47,836 --> 00:09:48,913
professional about.

243
00:09:48,953 --> 00:09:51,439
But for the everyday version, it's a useful

244
00:09:51,479 --> 00:09:52,652
reality check.

245
00:09:53,702 --> 00:09:55,568
So, back to that room at Cornell.

246
00:09:55,608 --> 00:09:58,050
One student, one embarrassing shirt, a room

247
00:09:58,090 --> 00:10:00,707
full of people… and most of them couldn't tell

248
00:10:00,747 --> 00:10:02,136
you who was on it.

249
00:10:03,186 --> 00:10:05,390
The spotlight you feel is real.

250
00:10:05,430 --> 00:10:07,706
It's just mostly pointed at you...

251
00:10:07,746 --> 00:10:08,603
by you.

252
00:10:09,653 --> 00:10:11,985
What's one moment you're still sure everyone

253
00:10:12,025 --> 00:10:13,764
noticed? Tell me in the comments.

254
00:10:13,804 --> 00:10:15,596
I bet it was smaller than it felt.

255
00:10:15,636 --> 00:10:18,022
And if this made a crowded room feel a little

256
00:10:18,062 --> 00:10:20,340
emptier, subscribe for more ways your brain

257
00:10:20,380 --> 00:10:21,808
gets in its own way.
